@layer 2
@aid_version 0.2
@purpose Recursive-descent parser with Pratt expression parsing that converts a token stream into a flat, index-addressed AST NodePool; supports Aria's full syntax (declarations, expressions, patterns, types) plus a fast declaration-index pass that scans decls without building the full AST.
@depends [lexer, diagnostic]

---

@workflow parse_pipeline
@purpose Token stream -> typed AST in a NodePool
@steps
  1. parse() initializes Parser with an empty NodePool and DiagnosticBag. [src: parser.aria:16-17]
  2. Skip the leading EOF sentinel and any module declaration. [src: parser.aria:126-133]
  3. Dispatch _parse_top for each top-level declaration in a loop. [src: parser.aria:171-176]
  4. Return a root EkBlock whose list indices point to all top-level decls. [src: parser.aria:188-189]

@workflow decl_index_fast_scan
@purpose Locate every top-level decl without building the AST — used for incremental builds
@steps
  1. Tokenize once without full parsing. [src: parser.aria:210-220]
  2. Skip pub / annotation prefixes, record decl boundaries. [src: parser.aria:276-298]
  3. Specialized skippers (_bdi_skip_braces / _bdi_skip_brackets / _bdi_skip_parens) traverse bodies without parsing. [src: parser.aria:627-653]
  4. Bridge back to the AST pool for name extraction. [src: parser.aria:568-570]

@workflow pratt_expression
@purpose Operator-precedence expression parsing
@steps
  1. Parse prefix/atom via _parse_prefix. [src: parser.aria:1817]
  2. Loop over postfix binding-power (field access, calls, index). [src: parser.aria:1833-1860]
  3. Loop over infix binding-power at min_bp threshold. [src: parser.aria:1862-1874]
  4. Recurse right with bp.right. [src: parser.aria:1869]

---

@invariants
  Parser position advances on every valid parse step. On token mismatch _expect records a diagnostic but leaves pos unchanged — callers must advance manually. [src: parser.aria:50-56]
  Every AST node lives in the NodePool; children are referenced by i64 indices (c0, c1, c2, list_start + list_count), never nested. [src: ast.aria:143-157]
  Pool index 0 is a sentinel EkNone so out-of-bounds access returns a safe value. [src: ast.aria:171-172, ast.aria:178-183]
  Binding powers: left-associative ops have right = left + 1; right-associative / prefix do not use the right field. [src: precedence.aria:10-17]
  Or-patterns in match arms are capped at 8 alternatives (hardcoded alt_idx_0..7). [src: parser.aria:2454-2476]

---

@antipatterns
  _expect() does NOT advance on mismatch. A caller that loops on _expect without verifying the position changed will spin until a separate _adv() call drives it to EOF. The infamous closure-expr case: `_expect(pp, "(")` at line 2813 — if `(` is missing the loop at 2815 iterates until EOF. Root-cause parity with the Go bootstrap parser's infinite-loop bug. [src: parser.aria:50-56, parser.aria:2813-2825]
  Storing pool indices as raw i64 (c0/c1/c2/list_start) means an off-by-one corrupts silently — index 0 returns the sentinel, other bad indices return the wrong node with no check. [src: ast.aria:150-155]
  Treating operator precedence dispatch as a map lookup: precedence.aria uses a long if-chain on token name strings for hot-path speed; replacing with a map would add hash overhead on every infix step. [src: precedence.aria:27-127]

